An Irq's hold on its notification is made a root rather than below the capability it was bound with,
so no revoke above that capability takes it, and the destroy of the notification's pool
leaves the Irq signalling memory that is no longer a notification;
an Irq's notification is never a root.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-smp2 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: capability to an object is a root

diff --git a/kernel/syscall.c b/kernel/syscall.c
--- a/kernel/syscall.c
+++ b/kernel/syscall.c
@@ -742,7 +742,7 @@ static int op_irq_line(struct thread *t, uint32_t slot, const struct cap *cap,
          */
         irq->ntfn = (struct cap){ .type = CAP_NOTIFICATION, .rights = nc.rights, .a = nc.a };
         irq->may = nc.b;
-        cap_attach(slot_node(t, arg[2]), &irq->ntfn);
+        cap_attach(NULL, &irq->ntfn);
         irq->line = first;
         /* A period counts from the last deadline, and a line that never fired from its bind. */
         irq->deadline = sched_ticks;
